Prompt:

[BRAND]: {brand name}
[PRODUCT]: {food / dish}
[HEADLINE]: {short bold word}
[INTERACTION]: {through counters / grip letters / wrap around strokes / fold through type}
[FOOD ELEMENT]: {salmon ribbon / octopus tentacle / citrus peel / pasta sheet / other suitable food}
[ACCENT COLOR]: {headline color}
[DETAILS]: {ingredients / short copy / hours / address}

Create a 9:16 vertical food editorial poster combining highly realistic food photography with large, professionally designed typography.

Use a warm cream or ivory background, subtle paper texture, clean editorial composition and minimal props.

Keep [HEADLINE] large, flat, sharp and fully readable. The food must remain a real independent ingredient and physically travel through the typography according to [INTERACTION].

For “through counters,” let the food clearly enter and exit the open spaces inside letters, with believable front and back occlusion.

For “grip letters,” let a natural part of the food physically attach to or hold a letter while the typography itself remains unchanged.

For “wrap around strokes,” use one continuous edible element to move in front of, behind and around different letter strokes with a traceable path.

For “fold through type,” use a broad flexible food sheet with visible thickness and real food texture, folding between the front, back and negative spaces of the letters.

Every interaction must have clear depth. Food in front of type should cast subtle contact shadows; food behind type should be genuinely hidden. Keep the food’s texture, thickness and direction continuous so the viewer can trace the same ingredient through the composition.

Do not turn food into letters, do not use food textures to fill typography, and do not rely on digital warp or fake 3D effects.

Use [ACCENT COLOR] with natural food colors and dark neutrals. Add only a few meaningful details such as [BRAND], product name, ingredients, one short line of copy, opening hours and address.

The final image should work as a stop-scroll poster at thumbnail size: one bold headline, one recognizable food element and one immediately understandable spatial interaction.

Prompt:

[SUBJECT]: {face / body / mask / sculpture / cultural object / product}
[TITLE]: {name}
[LINE FIELD]: {parallel / elliptical / concentric / radial / flowing contour}
[BACKGROUND]: {color}
[LINES]: {line color}
[TEXT]: {title + 2 to 4 short information lines}
[FORMAT]: 9:16 vertical

Create a refined experimental poster where [SUBJECT] is formed almost entirely through a dense field of precise continuous lines.

Use one coherent [LINE FIELD] system across the whole composition. Keep the lines extremely fine, clean, smooth, evenly spaced, and visually consistent.

Do not place a realistic subject on top of the lines. Let the subject emerge from the deformation of the line field itself.

As the lines approach the subject, let them gradually compress, expand, bend, and flow around its volume. Use only a few large, controlled deformations instead of many small ripples.

Keep the subject recognizable, but minimal. Faces, bodies, masks, and objects should be defined mainly through line curvature, spacing, and negative space rather than realistic textures or separate outlines.

Away from the subject, the lines should gradually return to their original geometric structure.

Keep the composition calm, elegant, and minimal. The main form should occupy roughly 65 to 80% of the image, with generous negative space.

Add only 2 to 3 small typography blocks using modern sans-serif type. Keep the text much smaller than the main visual.

Style: Op Art, generative graphic design, modern sculpture, experimental editorial poster, refined minimalism.

Highest priority: line quality.

Every line should stay sharp, continuous, smooth, elegant, and traceable through the deformation. Avoid broken lines, intersections, branching, jagged curves, uneven spacing, tangled areas, chaotic ripples, realistic surface textures, heavy 3D rendering, sci-fi UI, logos, numbering, starbursts, sparkles, and unnecessary decoration.

Prompt:

[SCENE]: {flower stall / confession / moving day / rooftop laundry / etc.}
[PEOPLE]: {age, relationship, appearance}
[ACTION]: {what is happening in this exact moment}
[ARCHITECTURE]: {shopfront / apartment entrance / rooftop / street corner}
[TITLE]: {short bold keyword}
[ACCENT COLOR]: {red / blue / green / etc.}
[SUBTITLE]: {short description}
[FORMAT]: 9:16

Create a vertical editorial poster built around a real everyday moment with visible people, natural interaction, and a distinctive architectural setting.

The image should feel like a genuine lifestyle photograph first and a designed poster second. Show the people actively doing something inside the scene, with readable faces, natural expressions, believable body language, and no obvious posing or camera awareness.

Use nearby architecture, furniture, objects, plants, signs, boxes, fabrics, flowers, or other real-life details to make the environment feel lived-in and visually rich.

Set “{TITLE}” in oversized bold condensed typography and integrate it directly into the scene. Let people, objects, doorways, awnings, fabric, walls, or architectural lines overlap, interrupt, frame, or pass in front of the letters. Typography should feel embedded in the real space rather than simply placed over the photo.

Use a restrained neutral palette with subtle paper and print texture, keeping the image clean rather than heavily distressed. Add {ACCENT COLOR} only as a small visual accent.

Add only a few meaningful editorial details such as thin lines, small labels, or graphic marks. Avoid random filler text, excessive technical information, numbering, logos, watermarks, sparkles, heavy grunge, and overly clean commercial-ad styling.

Overall style: lifestyle photography × editorial poster design × experimental typography × subtle neo-retro print texture.

Prompt:

Create a 9:16 editorial food poster based on the following inputs:

Brand: {brand name}
Food / Drink: {main product}
Headline: {main campaign line}
Supporting text: {product name / ingredients / price / opening hours}
Background color: {light neutral or soft brand color}
Accent colors: {1–2 restrained accent colors}
Typography word: {one short word related to the product}
Composition direction: {vertical / diagonal / circular / crossed / horizontal}

Build the entire poster around the natural shape and direction of the food itself.

Use one clear hero product with highly realistic food photography: appetizing textures, natural highlights, shadows, moisture, crumbs, glaze, melted cheese, glass reflections, fresh ingredients, or other details appropriate to the product. Avoid CGI, plastic textures, excessive retouching, or generic e-commerce photography.

Let the product shape determine how the layout moves. A tall drink can drive a vertical composition, a pizza slice can create a strong diagonal, a round bowl can guide circular typography, and crossed products can form an X-shaped structure.

Use oversized editorial typography as an active part of the composition. The main word should be very large, cropped by the canvas, partially hidden behind the food, split across different areas, rotated, curved, or extended beyond the frame according to the product geometry.

The food and typography must overlap and interact. Do not simply place text beside the product. Let the product cover parts of letters, cross typographic boundaries, break through color blocks, or interrupt the grid so photography and graphic design feel integrated.

Use a clear hierarchy:
oversized display typography,
hero food photography,
main headline,
product information,
price / time / short supporting details.

Keep all supporting copy meaningful and concise. Do not use Lorem Ipsum or random filler text.

Use a restrained color system with a light background, realistic food colors, and 1–2 brand accents. Add only a few graphic elements such as thin lines, dots, open frames, geometric blocks, soft organic shapes, or small information labels.

The composition should feel energetic and asymmetric while still carefully art-directed, with enough negative space to keep the poster readable.

Final style: modern food editorial design, bold typography, dynamic composition, photorealistic food photography, retro-modern branding, clean spacing, playful but professional.

