Prompt:

[SUBJECT]: {female profile / running shoe / ceramic vase / perfume bottle / architecture / letterform...}
[TITLE]: {name}
[BACKGROUND]: {color}
[RASTER LINES]: {line color}
[DIRECTION]: {horizontal / vertical / diagonal}
[TEXT]: {title + 2–4 short information lines}
[FORMAT]: 9:16 vertical

Design an experimental poster using a continuous high-density raster line field, large-scale spatial deformation, and negative-space typography.

Cover the entire composition with fine parallel lines. Keep every line clean, continuous, smooth, uniform in thickness, and consistently spaced. The lines should form one coherent field with no breaks, intersections, sudden bends, or random short fragments.

Place [SUBJECT] as the main visual, occupying roughly 70–80% of the composition. Do not simply place the subject on top of the lines. Instead, make it feel like a hidden 3D form pushing through the raster field.

Use only 2–4 large, controlled deformation zones rather than many small ripples. Let the lines smoothly compress, expand, bend, and flow around the subject, then gradually return to the original parallel field away from it.

The subject should become visible mainly through deformation and negative space. Keep only minimal real details where needed for recognition.

Create 2–3 small information areas inside quieter negative spaces. Use minimal modern sans-serif typography with strong hierarchy and generous spacing. Keep the text much smaller than the main visual and avoid placing it over dense line areas.

Style: Op Art, generative graphic design, experimental typography, contemporary editorial poster, refined and minimal.

Prioritize line quality above everything else: extremely fine, sharp, elegant, uninterrupted curves with smooth deformation and precise spacing.

Avoid broken lines, warped or jagged curves, intersections, chaotic ripples, excessive realism, generic sci-fi UI, cyberpunk effects, excessive text, logos, numbering, starbursts, and sparkles.

Prompt:

[PRODUCT / MATERIAL]: {red wine / coffee / sparkling water / ink / ...}
[BRAND]: {brand name}
[PRODUCT NAME]: {product name}
[CONTAINER]: {bottle / pouch / can / dropper / ...}
[PALETTE]: {background + liquid + typography colors}
[LIQUID BEHAVIOR]: {splash / bubbles / swirl / diffusion / refraction / ...}
[INFO TEXT]: {2–4 short product lines}
[FORMAT]: 9:16 vertical

Create a refined experimental typography poster where letters behave like real physical objects interacting with liquid.

Place the {CONTAINER} near the top edge, partially cropped and tilted downward. Let letters from {BRAND} and {PRODUCT NAME} pour out, fall through generous negative space, and enter {PRODUCT / MATERIAL}.

Give the falling letters controlled randomness through varied scale, rotation, spacing, overlap and depth. Keep a clear downward motion, with the type becoming denser near the liquid surface.

Make the liquid boundary the main visual event. As letters enter the liquid, apply {LIQUID BEHAVIOR} through realistic refraction, bubbles, drag, partial occlusion, distortion or fluid diffusion. Some letters can cross the surface, with their underwater portions visibly affected by the medium.

Follow a clear visual rhythm:

release → fall → gather → impact → transform

Keep splashes elegant and restrained. Use transparent and concentrated liquid layers, subtle depth, and visible negative space rather than heavy fluid effects.

Use {PALETTE} to create a slight contrast between the upper air space and lower liquid space. Add only a small editorial information area using {INFO TEXT}.

Overall look: minimal, premium, editorial, experimental, physically believable and detailed up close while remaining clear at thumbnail size.

Avoid heavy 3D type, random scattered letters, excessive copy, oversized splashes, clutter, or generic commercial-ad styling.

Prompt:

[LETTER]: {T / Y / P / E / any letter}
[SPATIAL ACTION]: {fold / curve / intersect / layer / twist}
[PAPER COLOR]: {ivory / muted blue / sage green / warm sand}
[ACCENT COLOR]: {burgundy / cobalt blue / olive / ochre}
[TITLE]: {e.g. P / CURVE}
[FORMAT]: 9:16 vertical

Create an experimental editorial typography poster where a large, clearly recognizable letter is physically formed from real sheets of matte paper.

Keep about 70–80% of the letter structure recognizable, then transform it through {SPATIAL ACTION}. Let the paper fold, curve, overlap, intersect, or move through different depths while preserving the essential anatomy of the letter.

Use thin, flexible paper rather than thick 3D blocks. Show realistic paper edges, subtle surface texture, natural bends, soft tension, and convincing deformation.

Use {PAPER COLOR} as the main paper tone with {ACCENT COLOR} appearing only where the structure changes — inside a fold, curve, intersection, cut edge, or deeper layer. Keep the palette muted and editorial, not brightly saturated.

Use strong directional studio lighting to create large graphic shadows across the paper and background. Let light and shadow become part of the composition while keeping the paper form readable.

Keep typography restrained. Use one medium-size title such as “{TITLE}”, one small series label, and only a minimal amount of micro text. Use modern Grotesk sans-serif typography with lighter weights, generous spacing, and soft charcoal or gray ink rather than heavy pure black.

Leave large areas of paper completely free of text so the material, color, form, and shadow remain the main visual focus.

Build clear foreground, middle ground, and background relationships. Allow parts of the paper letter to extend beyond the frame for scale and tension.

Overall aesthetic: experimental typography, Swiss editorial design, paper sculpture, modern graphic design, restrained color, high-end art direction, realistic studio photography.

